  {
   "topic": "Gate drive",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Threshold is 0.6 V to 1.6 V (1.0 V typical) at 250 µA with gate tied to drain, and it moves by 4.5 mV per ℃ typical. On-resistance is specified at two drive levels: 35 mΩ typical, 50 mΩ max at 10 V and 3 A; 50 mΩ typical, 60 mΩ max at 4.5 V and 2 A. The gate takes ±20 V and leaks under ±100 nA at that level. Bringing the gate to 4.5 V at 20 V and 2 A takes 6.5 nC typical, 12.5 nC max; internal gate resistance is 1.7 Ω typical. At VDS = 15 V and ID = 3 A the gate voltage plateaus near 1.3 V from about 0.4 nC to 2 nC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switching and gate-charge definitions",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Switching times are taken between the 10 % and 90 % points of the gate and drain waveforms: turn-on delay runs from the gate passing 10 % to the drain passing 90 % on its way down, rise time from 90 % to 10 % of the drain fall; turn-off delay runs from the gate falling through 90 % to the drain passing 10 % on its way up, fall time from 10 % to 90 % of the drain rise. Ton is delay plus rise; Toff is delay plus fall. Qg is the charge to bring the gate to 4.5 V; Qgs is the charge up to the start of the plateau and Qgd the width of the plateau."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Thermal figures are for the part soldered to a 1 in² pad of 2 oz copper on FR-4 for under 10 s: junction to ambient 125 ℃/W max, junction to case 75 ℃/W max. Dissipation is 1.0 W at 25 ℃ ambient, limited by the 150 ℃ junction. Continuous drain current at 4.5 V of drive is 5.8 A at 25 ℃ case and 2.5 A at 70 ℃ case. On-resistance at 150 ℃ is about 1.7 times its 25 ℃ value, and threshold at 150 ℃ is under 0.4 times its 25 ℃ value."
  },
